  ('cvh-026-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 26, 1, NULL, 'The verb to have is sometimes expressed by inverting the sentence and placing the nominative between the words HAI 喺 and ch''ü 處; e.g. the man has the basin becomes: the basin is (at) the man''s place 面盆喺男人處 MIN P''un HAI NAM YAN CH''ü.

The word call and tell (order) are expressed by the same word in Chinese: e.g. KIU k''ü 叫佢 is either tell him or call him. A distinction is sometimes made by the use of the words WA k''ü TING 話佢聽 to signify tell him, (lit. tell him to know.)

The past participle is formed by the addition of kwo 過, or hiu 嘵; thus to go is hui 去; gone is hui kwo or hui hiu, 去過，去嘵. The past form, however, is often dispensed with in actual practice, the context being deemed sufficient to shew that it is implied.

Ho 好 good, is frequently prefixed to adjectives to express very; e.g. HO TAI好大very large.

The plural forms of thus and that, NI TI 呢啲 KO TI 响啲 are frequently used when speaking of liquids, or with words which take no distinction of number, such as weather, &c.

1 Where is that man? 响個人喺邊處 ko ko yan hai pin ch''ü?

2 He is upstairs 佢喺樓上 k''ü hai lau sheung

4 That man is good; that wo- 响個人好, 响個女 ko ko yan ho, ko ko nüi-yan shap-man is very bad 人十牙唔好 fan ''m ho

5 Call the chair coolie 叫抬轎佬來 kiu t''oi kiu-lo loi

6 Please bring nine large tea cups 多煩擰九隻大茶杯來 to fun ning kau chek tai ch''a-pui loi

7 I want ten chairs 我要十張椅 ngo iu shap cheung i

8 Is the good table here? 响張好檯喺呢處唔係呢 ko cheung ho t''oi hai ni ch''ü ''m ni?

9 These men are good; those呢啲男人好，响啲 ni ti nam-yan ho, ko ti nüi-yan women are bad女人唔好 ''m ho

11 He is gone to call the house-coolie 佢去叫管店來 k''ü hü kiu kun-tim loi

10 Where is the cook? 火頭喺邊處呢 fo-t''au hai pin ch''ü ni?

12 Don''t take away the tea 咪擰响啲茶去 mai ning ko ti ch''a hü

13 Put nine tea-cups inside 放九隻茶杯在裡頭 fong kau chek ch''a-pui tsoi lui t''au

14 Put the large hasin on the 放响個大面盆在

