  ('cvh-027-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 27, 2, 'LESSON VII.', '<table><tr><td>Paper</td><td>紙</td><td>chi&#x27;</td><td>Tea-pot</td><td>茶壺</td><td>ch&#x27;a-oo</td></tr><tr><td>Pen</td><td>枝筆</td><td>(chi) pǎt</td><td>Water-bottle</td><td>水樽</td><td>shui&#x27; tsun</td></tr><tr><td>Ink (foreign)</td><td>墨水</td><td>mǎk-shui&#x27;</td><td>To use</td><td>使用</td><td>shai&#x27; (or) yung&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Book</td><td>部書</td><td>(po&#x27;) shu&#x27;</td><td>With</td><td>同</td><td>t&#x27;ung</td></tr><tr><td>Clothes-brush</td><td>衣擦</td><td>i ts&#x27;āt</td><td>Twelve</td><td>十二</td><td>shap yi&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Tell, (order)</td><td>話, 叫</td><td>wa&#x27;, kiu&#x27;</td><td>Twenty</td><td>二十</td><td>yi&#x27; shap</td></tr></table>', '<table><tr><td>Paper</td><td>紙</td><td>chi&#x27;</td><td>Tea-pot</td><td>茶壺</td><td>ch&#x27;a-oo</td></tr><tr><td>Pen</td><td>枝筆</td><td>(chi)pǎt</td><td>Water-bottle</td><td>水樽</td><td>shui&#x27;tsun</td></tr><tr><td>Ink(foreign)</td><td>墨水</td><td>mǎk-shui&#x27;</td><td>Touse</td><td>使用</td><td>shai&#x27;(or)yung&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Book</td><td>部書</td><td>(po&#x27;)shu&#x27;</td><td>With</td><td>同</td><td>t&#x27;ung</td></tr><tr><td>Clothes-brush</td><td>衣擦</td><td>its&#x27;āt</td><td>Twelve</td><td>十二</td><td>shapyi&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>Tell,(order)</td><td>話,叫</td><td>wa&#x27;,kiu&#x27;</td><td>Twenty</td><td>二十</td><td>yi&#x27;shap</td></tr></table>', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-028-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 28, 1, NULL, 'Hair-brush 擦頭掃 ts''at-t''au-so Fifty 五十 ''ng'' shap

Broom 掃把 so''-pa'' Much, many 多 to

To sweep 掃 so How many? 幾多 ki to?

Floor 樓板 lau-pan''

The numbers from eleven to nineteen form regularly, as: ten-one (11), ten-two (12), &c. From twenty to ninety they form similarly, as: two-ten (20), three-ten (30), and so on.

WA—CUI 話一知 is used for tell, when that word is intended to be used in a non-imperative sense.

Ki 幾 in ki to 幾多, how many, is literally a few, several. The two words in conjunction are, however, not always used in an interrogative sense, but sometimes answer to "a certain number;" as NGO MO KI TO, 我冇幾多, I have not many.

1 Bring a broom 擰把掃把來 ning pa so-pa loi

2 Sweep the floor 掃樓板 so lau pan

3 Give me pen and ink 俾筆俾墨水我 pi pät pi mak-shui ngo

4 Get a clothes-brush 擰衣擦來 ning i-ts‘at loi

