  ('cvh-069-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 69, 1, 'CLASSIFIERS.', 'Attention has already been drawn to the Classifiers, and their use explained. (Vide Lesson III and note.) As a perfect knowledge of them is a great aid to correct speaking, the learner will do well to commit to memory the lists given in this and following lessons.

1. Ko'' 個, a thing, is, strictly speaking, applied to men and ko 簡, a bamboo stalk, to inanimate objects; the latter may often be used with other substantives when the correct classifier is unknown; the contracted form of 簡 is 个 and it is usually so written in manuscript.

2. Chek 隻, single, is applied to boats or ships, birds, quadrupeds and the limbs of the body.

3. Tuí 對, a pair, is applied to all articles necessarily used in pairs; such as shoes, stockings, &c.

4. Sheung 雙, a couple, is applied to articles not necessarily pairs but dual; TUI 對 and SHEUNG 雙 are sometimes interchanged.

5. Pa'' 把, to grasp, is applied to articles that can be grasped, such as a knife, umbrella, &c.

6. Cheung 張, to spread out, is applied chiefly to things that are spread out, such as sheets, table-covers, mats, &c.

The classifier invariably follows the numeral and precedes the adjective, in sentences where both are expressed; e.g. One good man YAT KO HO YAN — 個好人.

GENDER.

Gender is only expressed when it is desired to distinguish the sexes. The words used are as follows.

For mankind nam 男 male nü'' 女 female

,, animals (generally) kung 公 ,, na'' 乸 ,,

,, birds hung 雄 ,, ts''z 雌 ,,

and kung 公 , mo'' 母 , are also used

,, quadrupeds mau'' 牡 ,, p''an'' 牝 ,,

A cock 一隻鷄公 yat chek kai-kung A pig 一隻猪公 yat chek chü-kung

A hen 一隻鷄乸 yat chek kai-na'' A sow 一隻猪乸 yat chek chü-na''', 'AttentionhasalreadybeendrawntotheClassifiers,andtheiruseexplained.(VideLessonIIIandnote.)Asaperfectknowledgeofthemisagreataidtocorrectspeaking,thelearnerwilldowelltocommittomemorythelistsgiveninthisandfollowinglessons.1.Ko''個,athing,is,strictlyspeaking,appliedtomenandko簡,abamboostalk,toinanimateobjects;thelattermayoftenbeusedwithothersubstantiveswhenthecorrectclassifierisunknown;thecontractedformof簡is个anditisusuallysowritteninmanuscript.2.Chek隻,single,isappliedtoboatsorships,birds,quadrupedsandthelimbsofthebody.3.Tuí對,apair,isappliedtoallarticlesnecessarilyusedinpairs;suchasshoes,stockings,&c.4.Sheung雙,acouple,isappliedtoarticlesnotnecessarilypairsbutdual;TUI對andSHEUNG雙aresometimesinterchanged.5.Pa''把,tograsp,isappliedtoarticlesthatcanbegrasped,suchasaknife,umbrella,&c.6.Cheung張,tospreadout,isappliedchieflytothingsthatarespreadout,suchassheets,table-covers,mats,&c.Theclassifierinvariablyfollowsthenumeralandprecedestheadjective,insentenceswherebothareexpressed;e.g.OnegoodmanYATKOHOYAN—個好人.GENDER.Genderisonlyexpressedwhenitisdesiredtodistinguishthesexes.Thewordsusedareasfollows.Formankindnam男malenü''女female,,animals(generally)kung公,,na''乸,,,,birdshung雄,,ts''z雌,,andkung公,mo''母,arealsoused,,quadrupedsmau''牡,,p''an''牝,,Acock一隻鷄公yatchekkai-kungApig一隻猪公yatchekchü-kungAhen一隻鷄乸yatchekkai-na''Asow一隻猪乸yatchekchü-na''', 'needs_review'),
