  ('cvh-102-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 102, 1, 'LESSON XXXIV.', '<table><tr><td>To travel by land</td><td>打路去</td><td>ta'' lo'' hü''</td><td>The world; empire</td><td>天下</td><td>t''in-ha''</td></tr><tr><td>Do.</td><td>行遊</td><td>hang-yau</td><td>Profit (money)</td><td>利</td><td>li''</td></tr><tr><td>Interest (of money)</td><td>利息</td><td>li''-sik</td><td>To invite</td><td>請</td><td>ts''ing''</td></tr><tr><td>A stove-bed</td><td>炕床</td><td>hong''-ch''ong</td><td>To worship</td><td>拜</td><td>pai''</td></tr><tr><td>News</td><td>新聞</td><td>sān-män</td><td>Ghosts</td><td>鬼</td><td>kwai''</td></tr><tr><td>Guest</td><td>人客</td><td>yan-hak</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>

Owing to the previously existing ignorance of Chinese geographers regarding foreign countries, most of words used to express both them and their inhabitants are "bastard," i.e. attempts for the most part to reproduce the sounds of the names phonetically or descriptively. The following are those most commonly used:—

<table border="1"><tr><td>Englishman</td><td>英吉利人</td><td>ying-kut-li yan</td><td>Annamite</td><td>安南人</td><td>on-nam yan</td></tr><tr><td>German</td><td>日耳曼人</td><td>yat-i&#x27;-man yan</td><td>Macaoese</td><td>澳門人</td><td>o&#x27;-mun yan</td></tr><tr><td>Frenchman</td><td>佛蘭西人</td><td>fat-lan-sai yan</td><td>Parsee</td><td>白頭人</td><td>pak-t&#x27;au yan</td></tr><tr><td>Spaniard</td><td>呂宋人</td><td>lü&#x27;sung&#x27; yan</td><td>American</td><td>花旗人</td><td>fa-k&#x27;i yan</td></tr><tr><td>Portuguese</td><td>西洋人</td><td>sai-yeung yan</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>

By substituting KWOK 國 for YAN 人 the name of the country is expressed, KWOK 國 meaning a state, country, kingdom. The word "world" above given, is in the Chinese classics used only to designate China, or the empire par excellence.

