  ('cvh-175-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 175, 1, NULL, '<table border="1"><tr><td>36</td><td>I cannot afford so much; the price is too dear</td><td>我唔出得咁多錢，價錢太貴</td><td>ngo ''m ch''ut tāk kom to ts''in；ka-ts''in t''ai kwai</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>(It is only) three dollars a bushel</td><td>三個銀錢一斗啫</td><td>sam ko ngăn-ts''in yat tau che</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>Did the compradore go away to worship the graves?</td><td>嗰個買辦去嘵拜山咩</td><td>ko-ko mai-pan hü hiu pai shan me?</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>I don''t know? I think he went last month</td><td>我唔知; 我估佢先個月去嘅咯</td><td>ngo ''m chi; ngo ku k''ü sin ko üt hü ke lok</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>Why are you always so very lazy?</td><td>爲乜野你時時咁懶惰呢</td><td>wai-mät-ye ni shi-shi kom lan-to ni?</td></tr></table>', '<tableborder="1"><tr><td>36</td><td>Icannotaffordsomuch;thepriceistoodear</td><td>我唔出得咁多錢，價錢太貴</td><td>ngo''mch''uttākkomtots''in；ka-ts''int''aikwai</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>(Itisonly)threedollarsabushel</td><td>三個銀錢一斗啫</td><td>samkongăn-ts''inyattauche</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>Didthecompradoregoawaytoworshipthegraves?</td><td>嗰個買辦去嘵拜山咩</td><td>ko-komai-panhühiupaishanme?</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>Idon''tknow?Ithinkhewentlastmonth</td><td>我唔知;我估佢先個月去嘅咯</td><td>ngo''mchi;ngokuk''üsinkoüthükelok</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>Whyareyoualwayssoverylazy?</td><td>爲乜野你時時咁懶惰呢</td><td>wai-mät-yenishi-shikomlan-toni?</td></tr></table>', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-175-02', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 175, 2, 'LESSON LIII.', 'ADVERBS AND ADVERBIAL EXPRESSIONS OF FUTURE TIME.

When is rendered by SHI 時 time, preceded by the sign of the possessive CHI 之, if used in a positive sense, and preceded by KI 幾 how much, how many, when used interrogatively.

e.g. When they were going out 佢哋去街之時 k''ü''-ti'' hü'' kai chi shü

When are you coming back? 你幾時翻來呢 ni'' ki'' shi fan loi ni?

Whenever is expressed by the sentence MO LUN KI SHI 無論幾時 (lit. no matter what time.)

As soon as {有咁快 yau'' kom''-fai'' Early to-morrow 明早 ming-tso''

得咁快 tāk kom''-fai'' Betime, early 早時 tso''-shi

A week hence {後一個 hau'' yat ko'' Afterwards 將來 tseung-loi

禮拜 lai''-pai'' Thereafter 後來 hau''-loi

Henceforward 自後 tsz''-hau'' By and by 慢慢 man''-man''

Alternately 輪流 lun-lau

Many of the above expressions have several equivalents, but they are not given, as it is better not to burden the student''s memory with too many words at', 'ADVERBSANDADVERBIALEXPRESSIONSOFFUTURETIME.WhenisrenderedbySHI時time,precededbythesignofthepossessiveCHI之,ifusedinapositivesense,andprecededbyKI幾howmuch,howmany,whenusedinterrogatively.e.g.Whentheyweregoingout佢哋去街之時k''ü''-ti''hü''kaichishüWhenareyoucomingback?你幾時翻來呢ni''ki''shifanloini?WheneverisexpressedbythesentenceMOLUNKISHI無論幾時(lit.nomatterwhattime.)Assoonas{有咁快yau''kom''-fai''Earlyto-morrow明早ming-tso''得咁快tākkom''-fai''Betime,early早時tso''-shiAweekhence{後一個hau''yatko''Afterwards將來tseung-loi禮拜lai''-pai''Thereafter後來hau''-loiHenceforward自後tsz''-hau''Byandby慢慢man''-man''Alternately輪流lun-lauManyoftheaboveexpressionshaveseveralequivalents,buttheyarenotgiven,asitisbetternottoburdenthestudent''smemorywithtoomanywordsat', 'verified'),
  ('cvh-176-01', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', 'canton-vernacular-handbook', '1874', 176, 1, NULL, 'a time. A reference to the vocabulary at the end of the book will frequently furnish synonyms, or a native teacher can readily supply them if desired; but some caution must be observed in accepting all phrases from such a source, many teachers using book sentences to an undue extent.

The phrase "as soon as" is often expressed by an idiom very similar to one found in English; e.g. "As soon as he heard it he went" is, literally, "one hear, then (he) went." YAT T''ENG TSAU HÜ 一聽就去. The English phrase "one blow, and all was over" embodies a similar elision, answering as it does to "(as soon as) one blow (had been struck) all was over."

